title: Justified Before Men – James 2
description: Justified before God by faith, and before men by works: James 2 read beside Romans 4, from the teachings on salvation.
output: lessons/doctrine/topic-salvation-justified-before-men.html
highlight: justifi

# Built from the teachings under "Justified before men: James 2" in studies/data/topics/salvation.json,
# with standalone teachings on the same theme. Slide text is the author's own sentences.

=== Two kinds of justification
# from 14024
--- Justified Before Men [title]
overline: Doctrine · Salvation
heading: Justified Before Men
sub: We are justified before God by our faith and justified before men by our works.
refs: James 2:18-23; Romans 4:1-5; 1 Samuel 16:7
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

# from 10779
--- No contradiction, only context
kicker: The doctrine
lead: By comparing scripture with scripture we see that there is no contradiction, only context.
fig: Before God | Romans 4:2-3
fig: Before men | James 2:18
p: Paul is writing of justification before God in grace, while James is writing of justification before man, by doing good works in faith.

# from 14024
--- The Lord looketh on the heart
big: 1 Samuel 16:7 | mark: man looketh on the outward appearance, but the LORD looketh on the heart
p: This is an important doctrine, that reinforces the seeming dichotomy of [[Romans 4:2-3|Romans 4]] and [[James 2:1|James 2]] that so many struggle with. That we are justified before God by our faith and justified before men by our works. Our appearance. That men are respecters of persons.
refs: James 2:1-4; John 7:24

=== Reading James 2
# from 3554, 10779
--- Written to the brethren
lead: It is written to the brethren, the author assumes it is to those already saved, to exhort and edify, not to get them to believe.
p: Not to save them spiritually, but to those already saved, become more complete in their Christian life.
callout: We do not receive doctrine from rhetorical questions or “a man may say…” reasoning, stripped of context. We get them from clear statements that do not contradict others.

# from 3555
--- Dead faith
scripture: James 2:14-17 | mark: faith, if it hath not works, is dead, being alone
p: If someone is needy and you basically just say “thoughts and prayers!” it doesn’t profit them anything. It’s useless. Dead. It doesn’t do anyone any good.
p: Dead faith doesn’t mean it doesn’t exist, but that there’s no life in it. Nothing to indicate it, outwardly. It’s alone, profiting no one else. A dead body exists, but it just lays there.

# from 3555
--- Doers of the word
lead: The example of this rhetorical question *says* he has faith. This is important.
p: The same is the faith of a Christian who doesn’t exercise it to help the fatherless and widows and the poor. See the theme? Understand the larger context?
p: We aren’t judged by our works and sin, as the debtors are. We are free in Christ. James has already told us that we earn crowns of life for our deeds. But salvation is a gift, unmerited. Grace-mixing isn’t how any of it works.
refs: James 1:12; James 1:22; James 1:27; James 2:12

# from 3557
--- A man may say
scripture: James 2:18-20 | mark: a man may say
p: A man may say this. What kind of man? Saved? Unsaved? A respecter of persons? Someone who could reasonably or unfairly doubt your profession of faith by your lack of visible works?
callout-honest: It is not directly stated doctrine. A man may say all manner of things. For example, we know devils are not capable of coming to saving belief in Christ. Yet here it says, in this hypothetical man’s argument, that they believe and tremble!

=== Abraham
# from 10779, 3557
--- Was not Abraham justified by works?
lit: The rhetorical question | James 2:21
rev: Not before God | Romans 4:2-3
p: The rhetorical question asked by a rhetorical man, “Was not Abraham justified by works?” is answered by the holy Spirit in [[Romans 4:1-5|Romans 4]] and [[Genesis 15:6|Genesis 15]]. Not before God, or he would have to glory. The boasting wouldn’t be excluded. The law of grace would become one of debt.

# from 3557
--- Justified before God, by faith alone
lead: Abraham was saved spiritually, justified before God, by his faith alone.
big: Genesis 15:6
p: Decades before his circumcision. Decades before bringing Isaac to the altar on the same mountain in Jerusalem where Jesus would later be crucified.
refs: Romans 4:9-11; Romans 4:5

# from 3557, 10779
--- Justified before men, by works
scripture: James 2:21-23 | mark: by works was faith made perfect
p: But to be justified before man, that outward sign of his faith, made his faith more perfect. There was a visible tangible act of faith, works that a man may say that they’ve been shown his faith.
note: A man may say that Abraham’s faith was shown in his works and thus he was justified to others. But we know that scripture says it was faith that justified Abraham to God.

# from 9230
--- The people justified God
big: Luke 7:29-30
p: Justified is also used in a legal sense, meaning vindication. To show that they believed the report, accepted the witness, agreed with the argument, they were baptized, justifying God.
callout: Baptism itself doesn’t save. It is a manifestation of the faith, the substance of things not seen, that can be seen of men.

=== Faith shown to men
# from 4116, 8722, 11746
--- Saved by faith, shown by works
lead: We are saved by faith alone. We share that faith by how we live. And by being mindful of that, we get others saved.
p: We may be justified by the blood of Christ before God, but if we hide that faith under a bushel, or we bury that which we’ve been trusted rather than gain an increase for the Lord, our faith is fruitless. Dead.
p: The works God brings us to aren’t of ourselves. They’re almost always a blessing for others.
refs: 2 Corinthians 5:1-3; 2 Corinthians 3:2; Matthew 5:16

# from 8722, 3554, 13927, 9563
--- The snare of James 2
lit: Ye are they which justify yourselves before men | Luke 16:15
panel-warn: Works for justification | Those that twist it to make works their means of justification before God, damn themselves and put Christ to an open shame, for their dead works are without faith.
panel: The answer | If you are trusting in your own works to get righteous or to maintain the righteousness given by God in Christ, you are damning yourself and being made debtor to the whole of the law which no one can keep perfectly. There are none righteous. Our only righteousness is imputed of Christ.
note: Repent, and obey the Gospel.

=== Summary
# from 14024, 10779, 3554, 3555, 3557, 4116
--- Justified before men
kicker: Summary
point: Two kinds | We are justified before God by our faith and justified before men by our works.
point: Context | Paul is writing of justification before God in grace, while James is writing of justification before man.
point: To the brethren | It is written to the brethren, to those already saved, to exhort and edify, not to get them to believe.
point: Dead faith | Dead faith doesn’t mean it doesn’t exist, but that there’s no life in it. It’s alone, profiting no one else.
point: Abraham | Justified before God, by his faith alone. To be justified before man, that outward sign of his faith, made his faith more perfect.
point: Shown by works | We are saved by faith alone. We share that faith by how we live.

